DevOps Pipeline Project Setup Documentation

Overview:

This document outlines the steps required to set up a DevOps pipeline involving Jenkins, 
SonarQube, Nexus, Docker, Kubernetes, and OWASP tools for continuous integration and deployment (CI/CD).

Prerequisites
Server or local machine with sufficient resources
Installed software:
Java JDK (version 8 or higher)
Docker
Kubernetes (Minikube, or access to a Kubernetes cluster)
Git
Maven or Gradle (depending on the project)


Step 1: Install Jenkins
Download Jenkins:

Visit Jenkins Downloads and download the appropriate installer for your OS.
Install Jenkins:

Follow the installation instructions based on your operating system.
Start Jenkins:

Run the Jenkins service.
Access Jenkins web interface from http://localhost:8080 (or the configured URL).
Unlock Jenkins:

Follow the prompts to unlock Jenkins using the initial admin password found in the specified path (usually /var/lib/jenkins/secrets/initialAdminPassword).
Install Suggested Plugins:

During the setup, install the suggested plugins or select specific plugins as listed in the next section.
Step 2: Install Required Plugins
Access Jenkins Dashboard > Manage Jenkins > Manage Plugins.
In the Available tab, search and install the following plugins:
Pipeline
Git
SonarQube Scanner for Jenkins
Nexus Platform Plugin
Docker Pipeline
Kubernetes Plugin
Blue Ocean
Email Extension Plugin
Slack Notification Plugin
Workspace Cleanup Plugin
OWASP Dependency-Check Plugin
OWASP ZAP Plugin
Step 3: Install and Configure SonarQube
Download SonarQube:

Visit SonarQube Downloads and download the Community Edition.
Install SonarQube:

Follow the installation instructions for your environment (usually involves unpacking the archive and starting the service).
Configure SonarQube:

Access SonarQube UI from http://localhost:9000.
Create an administrator account and project.
Generate Authentication Token:

Go to "My Account" > "Security" to generate a token for Jenkins integration.
Step 4: Install and Configure Nexus
Download Nexus Repository Manager:

Visit Sonatype Nexus Downloads and download the version you need.
Install Nexus:

Follow the installation instructions provided on their website.
Configure Nexus:

Access Nexus UI from http://localhost:8081.
Set up hosted repositories for Docker and Maven artifacts.
Step 5: Install and Configure Docker
Install Docker:

Follow the installation guide suitable for your OS from the Docker Official Documentation.
Test Docker Installation:

Run docker --version in the terminal to verify the installation.
Step 6: Install and Configure Kubernetes
Install Minikube (or access to a Kubernetes cluster):

Follow the Minikube installation instructions from the Minikube Documentation.
Start Minikube:

Run minikube start in the terminal.
Verify Kubernetes Setup:

Use kubectl get nodes to check if your Kubernetes cluster is running.
Step 7: Set Up Pipeline in Jenkins
Create a New Pipeline Project:

From the Jenkins dashboard, click on "New Item" and select "Pipeline".
Configure SCM:

Set the repository URL and credentials for your source code.
Define Pipeline Script:

You can define the pipeline in the Jenkinsfile located in your repository or directly in the Jenkins UI.
Add Stages for Build, Test, and Deploy:

Integrate SonarQube analysis, Docker build, Nexus upload, and Kubernetes deployment in the pipeline script.
Step 8: Integrate OWASP Tools
Configure OWASP Dependency-Check Plugin:

Add a build step in Jenkins to run the dependency-check command to scan for vulnerabilities.
Integrate OWASP ZAP:

Add a post-deployment step to run OWASP ZAP against the application.
Step 9: Test the Pipeline
Run the Jenkins Pipeline:

Trigger the job manually or configure webhook triggers for automated builds.
Monitor the Build:

Check the Jenkins console output for logs, errors, and the status of each stage.
Review Results:

Access SonarQube dashboard for code quality reports.
Check Nexus for uploaded artifacts.
Verify deployed application in the Kubernetes cluster.
Conclusion
Following these steps, you will set up a comprehensive DevOps pipeline integrating Jenkins, SonarQube, Nexus, Docker, Kubernetes, and OWASP tools. 
This configuration helps streamline your CI/CD process and enhances the overall quality and security of your applications.
